=== layout: two-panels


# One Meiosis, One Ascus

@left

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect09b-tetrads_1_introduction/image_009.jpg)

@right

- *Ascomycete fungi form an* [*ascus*]{color=red} *with four haploid cells from meiosis*

- Formation of an ascus containing all of the four products of a single meiosis.

=== layout: one-panel

# Why Keep the Four Products Together?

@body

- Each ascus keeps the four products of a single meiosis together.
- Each haploid spore can grow by mitosis.
- Its phenotype directly reveals its genotype; dominance does not hide the alleles.
- In unordered tetrads, the positions of the four spores do not preserve meiotic order.

@notes

The yeast mating types a and alpha describe mating compatibility. They are separate from the mutant allele symbols used in the mapping problems.

=== layout: reference

# Yeast Life Cycle Reference

@body

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect09b-tetrads_1_introduction/image_005.jpg)


=== layout: bookend

# Key Takeaways

@body

- Compatible haploid yeast cells mate to form a diploid cell.
- Meiosis produces four haploid spores inside one ascus.
- An unordered tetrad retains all four products without preserving their meiotic order.

